WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.930
The Marvel Cinematic Universe is filled with
mind-blowing camera work and staggering visual

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:05.930
effects.

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:09.740
But there are a handful of particularly powerful
shots that truly stand out as being some of

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:14.820
the most ambitious, and successful, ones in
the entire superhero extravaganza to-date.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.610
Let's take a look at some of the Marvel movie
shots that have helped to define the franchise.

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:20.840
Always angry

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:25.550
The Avengers' first true assembly scene marked
the exact moment the MCU became the cinematic

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:28.620
juggernaut its architects always knew it would
be.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:32.759
"That's my secret, Cap.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.220
I'm always angry."

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:42.620
Banner's transformation is a direct lead-in
to the squad finally taking shape, after five

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:46.829
movies' worth of buildup, to battle what is
arguably still the most compelling villain

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:48.929
of the MCU, Loki.

00:00:48.929 --> 00:00:52.969
When people think back to The Avengers, they
think about this shot and the bevy of amazing

00:00:52.969 --> 00:00:55.059
scenes that follow it.

00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:56.359
Passenger patrol

00:00:56.359 --> 00:01:01.280
Iron Man 3's entire Air Force One rescue scene
is downright incredible, so narrowing it down

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.019
to a single shot was tough.

00:01:03.019 --> 00:01:06.961
While there are several examples of stunning
cinematography on display in this scene, one

00:01:06.961 --> 00:01:09.330
narrowly rises above the competition.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:13.360
It's the shot of Iron Man dashing down from
a destroyed Air Force One to save its entire

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.390
crew, immediately after being told by Jarvis
that carrying them all would be an impossibility.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:22.850
From a visual imagery perspective, the shot
contains all the necessary elements: a smoking

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:27.561
Air Force One in the distance, a crew of people
screaming and flailing mid-free fall, and

00:01:27.561 --> 00:01:31.330
a tiny gold and red speck rocketing toward
them to save the day.

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:35.580
This shot does an impeccable job of conveying
the stakes of the situation before Iron Man

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.540
defies all odds once again with another stroke
of brilliance.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:39.540
"Excellent.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.220
Good team effort all around.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:42.360
Go us."

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.740
Guardians gather

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:49.020
With Guardians of the Galaxy, James Gunn crafted
an action film that had heart and wit, and

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:52.920
the shot of the group getting together to
form their own mini-Avengers pack was so very

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:53.920
satisfying.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.570
The slow-mo dolly shot that aligns this new,
unusual gang of heroes is as spectacular as

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:03.150
it is subversive, with each of the five looking
annoyed or bored, or, in the case of Rocket

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:06.400
Raccoon, tugging on a suit too tight around
his junk.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.440
Its gritty lighting and muted colors, in an
otherwise colorful outer space world, gives

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.270
us the sense our heroes are walking toward
their doom.

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:16.780
And, considering a couple of them are yawning,
they're obviously not too busted up about

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:17.780
it.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.850
That tracking shot

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:23.660
Considering how exhilarating the first Avengers'
big tracking shot was, it's no surprise that

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:27.910
Joss Whedon opted to double-dip and reuse
the technique for the opening of Age of Ultron.

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:32.500
Here, he takes the idea to its logical extremes,
reserving the whole first minute of the film

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.550
for a non-stop assault on the senses.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:39.230
The camera moves at a rip-roaring pace, re-introducing
you to each Avenger and the abilities they

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:40.360
bring to the team.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:44.560
It's a near-perfect blend of pure spectacle
and unspoken character descriptions.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:48.800
And as icing on the cake, the shot even finishes
with the whole team assembled, striking a

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.459
pose together in mid-air that pays homage
to a number of comic covers..

00:02:52.459 --> 00:02:56.560
It's the ideal way to open up an Avengers
movie and get the audience ready for two hours

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.380
of high-octane, over-the-top action.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:01.110
The big punch

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:05.720
The Hulk vs. Hulkbuster fight instantly became
one of the most anticipated MCU throwdowns

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:08.320
of all time, and the hype was certainly well
warranted.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.920
This shot that really captures the most epic
moment is when the upper-hand flip-flops back

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.900
and forth between Hulk and Iron Man in his
Hulkbuster suit.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:20.380
The ultimate stalemate is reached when green
fist meets robot fist, creating a blast wave

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:22.470
that breaks glass and sends people flying.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:27.700
It's as intense as punches come and is captured
at an extra wide angle that really helps translate

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:29.530
the enormity of the two titans clashing.

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:33.690
Not only is it a very cool shot on a technical
level, but, for Marvel fans, it's just an

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:38.380
amazing sight to see Hulk and Iron Man going
at it on the big screen.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:39.380
Pistol hop

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:43.349
Maybe it's the kinetic energy of the camera
sweeping beside Ant-Man that makes the moment

00:03:43.349 --> 00:03:47.760
so special, but the shot of the title character
running on the surface of a gun immediately

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.800
after dodging its bullet at (literal) point-blank
range is superb.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.130
It's that brand of absurdity that perfectly
summarizes why the shot works: it revels in

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:59.620
its craziness while also giving Scott Lang
the heroic framing he deserves, however small

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:01.370
that might be.

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:02.910
Civil War

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:07.160
As a tribute to Captain America: Civil War's
source material, the climactic wide shot of

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.550
Cap fighting Iron Man is ripped directly from
the comics.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:14.630
In the scene, Cap charges at Stark, deflecting
each and every repulsor blast with his shield

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.769
until the two foes are inches apart.

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:20.769
Then the world slows to a crawl, isolating
the visually stunning moment when Iron Man

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:25.060
unleashes one final repulsor blast directly
onto the surface of Cap's shield.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:28.820
The blast reflects off it and blazes to life,
filling the screen with a light that could

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:32.900
only be achieved by the union of this unstoppable
force meeting the immovable object.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:37.430
There's no mystery why this shot is the defining
image of the film it's representing—it fulfills

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:41.410
everything we hoped this movie would be, all
within a few frames.

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:43.570
Kaecilius' Sling Ring Escape

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:47.670
You can tell the director and storyboard artist
had a fun time thinking up the various shots

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:52.121
in Doctor Strange, and the one that really
brings magic to the MCU is Kaecilius's big

00:04:52.121 --> 00:04:53.410
sling ring escape.

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:58.400
The mind-bending shot subliminally reinforces
one of Dr. Strange's biggest themes, that

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.200
everything's just a matter of perspective.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:04.940
The color grading leaves the buildings a dull,
desaturated grey divided only by pitch-black

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:08.990
shadows, allowing us to perfectly track where
the sorcerers are distorting geography and

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:12.410
help us focus our attention on the mystical
glowing elements.

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:17.160
It's also ridiculously busy yet super focused,
allowing us to be overwhelmed by the buildings

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:21.240
consuming themselves in the background while
still letting us remain fully concentrated

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:24.050
on Kaecilius' sling ring escape.

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:25.050
Ferry save

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:30.490
Last but not least, Spider-Man: Homecoming
introduced the quintessential hero shot, displaying

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:34.370
a protagonist challenging impossible odds
head-on in order to save the day.

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:38.100
There's nothing quite as epic as seeing the
teen hero desperately clutching to his webs

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.070
to keep the Staten Island Ferry and all of
its passengers from drowning.

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:45.150
Even though the scene was spoiled in the film's
trailers, it still represents the Spider-Man

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:48.320
ethos: utter determination even in the absence
of hope.

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:52.470
This moment reminds us that Spidey is always
in it for the greater good, which is why he's

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:53.840
one of the greatest heroes.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:55.030
Thanks for watching!

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:58.190
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:00.550
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

